A||N||9||bHooper||a question about the half shafts... or two...||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-04-2000||06:22 PM||||Now that I have the tranny on the engine, what is the best and easiest method for removeing the half shafts from the struts, so that I can mount them into the tranny?<P>If you can be detailed (in idiot's terms) that would even be better, as I am definately <B>not</B> a mechanic.  But i am becomeing one!<P>Thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||SCCA FIERO||05-04-2000||08:05 PM||||Is it possible to get them in the tranny without taking them out of the hub? I would try that so you don't get crud in the bearings. <P>BTW, the axles just slide into the tranny (you may have known that)||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||05-04-2000||09:56 PM||||With a little patience, and an assistant, you can put them in still attached to the spindle/strut assembly,, and save the cost of an alignment.  I did this when I changed my clutch.  I separated the spindle from the control arm, but you have to be careful not to damage the ball joint or strut boots.  I bolted the strut top to the body (loosely) first.  Here is where an assistant helps, obviously.  The axle seats solidly, and you can probably see the seal marks on it to tell you when it's in all the way.  <BR>I hope you put in new axle seals.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||05-04-2000||10:42 PM||||believe me, if there is a way to do that... i will make it happen!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>2birds, so you left the half shaft, attached (in its entireity (sp)) to the strut... attached the strut to the car (letting it support it) and then how did you force the axle into the seal?  George says that it needs to be pounded with a rubber malet.<P>After reading your post, i think it would be possible to attach the strats to the car... place the axle in front of the seal, and leaving the cradle and engine on the palet, raise the car again... one would think that this would cause the shaft to pop into the tranny (as it straighten outs).  then repeat the process for the other side.<P>Any thoughts?  I know it doesnt seem like the most scientific method, but.........||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||05-04-2000||11:11 PM||||I seem to be violating the ten second rule here.  Am I correct in understanding that you dropped the whole engine/cradle as an assembly?  I did mine with the engine held up by a cross bar, as shown in the chilton/haynes manuals.  If you dropped the whole assembly down, why would you have to remove the axles in the first place?  If it's all on the ground now, it sould be easy to pop them in though.  Just watch the cv joint angles don't get too big.<BR>Either way, slow and steady wins.  I have yet to use the BFH on mine, although I've been tempted a few times.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||05-05-2000||10:09 AM||||Remove the pinch bolt from the ball joint clamp on the bottom of the hub carrier. push the A arm down. this will give enough freedom to work but not screw up the alignment. You do not need to disconnect the strut or the tie rod. <P>I use this method on nearly all Fiero and FWD axle work. It's a bit of a crab to deal with if you have a sway bar involved but it works every time. (On a big sway bar you may have to disconnect an endlink to get enough movement in the arm. I can just barely do a Caravan with the bar connected.) Remember that for this type work Fiero is an FWD setup. Struts are built to turn. You won't hurt them or the tie rod by rotating the hub carrier out to clear the axle. As long as only the pinch bolt is removed there is no way to mess up the alighnment.||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||05-05-2000||10:12 AM||||ok...?... so what does a "pinch bolt" look like?  and does this release the shaft from the ball joint?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||05-05-2000||12:04 PM||||Yes, this is a horizontal bolt on the lower part of the spindle that holds the ball joint on. Ogre is telling you to drop that ball joint and control arm down out of the way off the spindle, but leave the strut and tie rods attached. This way you can line up the axles better and not mess up your alignment. The axles should just push in until you hear a definite snap or click. Don't forget to replace the axle seals first.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||05-05-2000||01:29 PM||||if by axle seals, you mean the ones in the tranny... those have been replaced.<P>i will look at this when i get home, this afternoon, to see if i understand it.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||05-06-2000||09:20 PM||||Ogre, Batboy... Thanks!  That work like a champ!  <P>and fortunate for me, that I looked at those bolts... one did not have a nut on it at all, the other, barely and half the threads were striped!  go figure, old cars :eek<P>thanks, again to everyone who helped with this!<P>hoop||||reg||1
